      "message": "**Issue:**\nIn both of the newly added tests (`test_fp_context_get_result_blocks_swap_under_auth` and `test_fp_template_v0_disabled_under_auth`), the test setup manually injects an active session flag to simulate a high-security state:\n`global_context.fp_encryption_status |\u003d FP_CONTEXT_STATUS_SESSION_ESTABLISHED;`\n\nHowever, the actual C++ implementation in the patch never checks for this flag. The new enforcement logic relies entirely on `fingerprint_auth_enabled()` and the `FP_CONTEXT_USER_ID_SET` flag.\n\nSetting `SESSION_ESTABLISHED` in the tests is dead code that creates a misleading impression of the underlying security invariants—it implies the lock-out only happens during an active session, rather than anytime FingerGuard is enabled. Furthermore, in `test_fp_template_v0_disabled_under_auth`, the inline comment explicitly states that it validates the block *\"regardless of whether an active session flag is present\"*, but then immediately sets the flag anyway.\n\n**Suggestion:**\nRemove the manipulation and cleanup of `FP_CONTEXT_STATUS_SESSION_ESTABLISHED` in both tests so they accurately reflect the implementation.\n\n* **In `test_fp_context_get_result_blocks_swap_under_auth`:** The prerequisite assertion `zassert_true(global_context.fp_encryption_status \u0026 FP_CONTEXT_USER_ID_SET);` (which is already set up by `fpsensor_before()`) is sufficient to trigger the new blocking logic. You can just rely on that.\n* **In `test_fp_template_v0_disabled_under_auth`:** Simply test the baseline where `fingerprint_auth_enabled()` is true without muddying the context state, perfectly matching your comment\u0027s intent.",
